Like, for many of us, we take education for granted.

00:16:25.690 --> 00:16:28.470
And for many of us, we just assume, well, of course, I'm going to go to high school.

00:16:28.550 --> 00:16:31.050
Of course, maybe I'm going to go to college, things like that.

00:16:31.310 --> 00:16:36.870
Back at that time, only about 1% of young Jewish men, men only, not women,

00:16:37.450 --> 00:16:42.870
only about 1% of young men received a formal Jewish education beyond an elementary level.

00:16:43.350 --> 00:16:46.830
For most people, by the time they were like past elementary years,

00:16:47.230 --> 00:16:48.910
well, then they were just learning a trade,

00:16:49.130 --> 00:16:50.810
or they were being apprenticed in a business,

00:16:50.930 --> 00:16:52.910
or they were just joining the family business.

00:16:53.130 --> 00:16:56.610
That's what they did, and they didn't have any further schooling beyond that.

00:16:56.830 --> 00:16:58.850
That's what a lot of Jesus' other disciples were like.

00:16:58.870 --> 00:17:00.910
They were like fishermen, right, doing that kind of stuff.

00:17:01.370 --> 00:17:02.890
Paul is one of that 1%,

00:17:02.890 --> 00:17:06.170
the people that would have gotten into the University of Michigan, right,

00:17:06.170 --> 00:17:09.850
people that get that elite education for him that was in Jerusalem

00:17:09.850 --> 00:17:15.310
under a teacher named Gamaliel, who taught him to be an expert in the laws of the Old Testament.

00:17:15.790 --> 00:17:19.710
And so he's received this Jewish education, and then because of that, he was able to excel

00:17:19.710 --> 00:17:24.850
as a Pharisee. And many of you know the Pharisees. These were people that they get a really bad rap

00:17:24.850 --> 00:17:29.110
in the New Testament. There are a lot of conflicts with the Pharisees and Jesus. But really at their

00:17:29.110 --> 00:17:33.790
core, these are people who are just really serious about understanding the Old Testament

00:17:33.790 --> 00:17:39.610
scriptures, wanted to be very intentional about not violating any of those laws or commandments,

00:17:39.850 --> 00:17:46.550
And they were so serious about this, they memorized and obeyed not only the 613 commandments

00:17:46.550 --> 00:17:51.950
in the Jewish Old Testament, but they also, on top of that, added hundreds or thousands

00:17:51.950 --> 00:17:56.290
of what were called fence laws that were intended to keep someone from even getting

00:17:56.290 --> 00:18:00.070
close or having the possibility of violating one of the commandments.

00:18:00.390 --> 00:18:05.370
So they added hundreds and thousands of commandments on top of the 613 that we see written in

00:18:05.370 --> 00:18:05.790
the scriptures.

00:18:06.210 --> 00:18:08.510
And Paul says, man, he excelled in all of them.

00:18:09.330 --> 00:18:10.930
He was really good at this stuff.

00:18:11.330 --> 00:18:13.910
Not just knowing it, but living it, obeying it.

00:18:14.170 --> 00:18:15.570
That was his life.

00:18:15.850 --> 00:18:17.410
And so as he excelled as a Pharisee,

00:18:17.490 --> 00:18:19.050
well, a result of that is he then ended up

00:18:19.050 --> 00:18:20.110
persecuting the church.

00:18:21.490 --> 00:18:24.530
He says, hey, there's some new teaching going on here.

00:18:25.010 --> 00:18:27.190
People are talking about this rabbi Jesus,

00:18:27.650 --> 00:18:29.670
and people are saying some things about him

00:18:29.670 --> 00:18:31.490
that I don't think are true, I don't think are good.

00:18:31.490 --> 00:18:33.210
He's leading people astray,

00:18:33.470 --> 00:18:35.750
and so he's persecuting the church.

00:18:36.590 --> 00:18:39.770
He's so serious and zealous about his faith.

00:18:40.190 --> 00:18:46.510
And so as we look at all of this, I mean, like Paul, Saul, he had the best education.

00:18:47.190 --> 00:18:48.510
He had career success.

00:18:49.210 --> 00:18:50.730
He had the admiration of others.

00:18:50.830 --> 00:18:54.090
He had the best passport to travel the world and go wherever he wanted to.

00:18:54.090 --> 00:18:58.410
But ultimately, he finds out that all of these are temporary pursuits.

00:18:59.530 --> 00:19:03.670
These are what the author of Ecclesiastes might have called chasing after the wind.

00:19:04.470 --> 00:19:06.170
It doesn't really go anywhere, right?

00:19:06.430 --> 00:19:08.570
And when does he discover this?

00:19:08.650 --> 00:19:09.970
When does he understand this?

00:19:10.650 --> 00:19:14.670
Is it in the classroom as he's just kind of going about his day,

00:19:14.830 --> 00:19:17.310
teaching his own students?

00:19:18.110 --> 00:19:21.450
Is it as he takes a special seminar about something about the real meaning of life?

00:19:21.870 --> 00:19:23.370
No, it's as he meets a person.

00:19:24.710 --> 00:19:27.870
We find out that ultimately, after all those sovereign foundations,

00:19:28.010 --> 00:19:30.670
he then encountered Christ on the road to Damascus.

00:19:31.210 --> 00:19:32.490
If you haven't read that, I would encourage you,

00:19:32.550 --> 00:19:34.670
go read Acts 9, 1-9.

00:19:35.230 --> 00:19:38.070
He encounters Jesus on the road to Damascus,

00:19:38.490 --> 00:19:39.990
and there he finds out

00:19:39.990 --> 00:19:42.830
that what all of these followers of the way,

00:19:42.930 --> 00:19:44.850
these Christians, what they've been talking about,

00:19:45.450 --> 00:19:48.410
that this rabbi died on the cross and rose again,

00:19:48.910 --> 00:19:51.610
that he did this to forgive people of their sins,

00:19:51.690 --> 00:19:52.910
to give them eternal life,

00:19:53.150 --> 00:19:55.350
this message he's been hearing and rejecting,

00:19:56.250 --> 00:19:58.470
he finds out that it's real, that it's true,

00:19:58.470 --> 00:20:01.230
that this is where you actually find eternal life.

00:20:01.970 --> 00:20:03.710
And as he has this encounter with Jesus,

00:20:03.830 --> 00:20:04.310
it's fascinating.

00:20:04.430 --> 00:20:07.370
It completely changes the direction of his life.

00:20:07.570 --> 00:20:08.710
And I think it's interesting to note

00:20:08.710 --> 00:20:10.370
that this is when he's about 30 years old.

00:20:11.650 --> 00:20:13.870
Paul would have been born slightly after Jesus.

00:20:14.410 --> 00:20:16.230
He's about 30 years old at this time.

00:20:16.430 --> 00:20:17.150
That's fascinating

00:20:17.150 --> 00:20:19.910
because most of Jesus' earlier followers were teenagers.

00:20:20.670 --> 00:20:23.630
We think probably only Peter was 20 years old or older

00:20:23.630 --> 00:20:25.750
because only Peter and Jesus had to pay the temple tax.

00:20:26.610 --> 00:20:30.250
So Paul, now he's 30 years old, and he meets Jesus,

00:20:30.690 --> 00:20:33.850
and this changes the entire course of his life.

00:20:34.390 --> 00:20:37.250
As he goes on then, we see he not only encountered Christ,

00:20:37.330 --> 00:20:39.710
but he then experienced God's grace in community.

00:20:40.090 --> 00:20:41.910
As he goes to the city of Damascus,

00:20:42.210 --> 00:20:45.490
there this guy named Ananias greets him and prays for him

00:20:45.490 --> 00:20:48.270
that the scales would fall off his eyes and his sight would be restored.

00:20:48.270 --> 00:20:52.010
He is welcomed into the community of the early church there in Damascus,

00:20:52.130 --> 00:20:54.910
and the same people that he went there to arrest

00:20:54.910 --> 00:20:57.310
and to carry back to Jerusalem in chains

00:20:57.310 --> 00:20:59.290
now are welcoming him, embracing him,

00:20:59.410 --> 00:21:01.030
protecting him, providing for him.

00:21:01.410 --> 00:21:02.830
He experiences that grace

00:21:02.830 --> 00:21:04.910
that even though he came there as their enemy,

00:21:05.190 --> 00:21:06.690
they embrace him as a brother.

00:21:07.170 --> 00:21:08.070
And as things continue,

00:21:08.190 --> 00:21:09.490
we then see that he goes out

00:21:09.490 --> 00:21:12.650
for a season of isolation in Arabia for three years.

00:21:13.550 --> 00:21:15.330
And so just as Jesus' earthly disciples

00:21:15.330 --> 00:21:17.410
got to spend three years with him doing ministry,

00:21:17.750 --> 00:21:19.510
Paul gets to go do that out in the desert,

00:21:19.510 --> 00:21:20.710
just communing with Jesus,

00:21:20.850 --> 00:21:22.450
getting to know him, hearing from him,

00:21:22.710 --> 00:21:24.730
learning more of him, all of that kind of stuff.

00:21:24.910 --> 00:21:26.970
he gets to do all of that.

00:21:27.190 --> 00:21:29.550
And then we find out he eventually goes to Jerusalem

00:21:29.550 --> 00:21:32.250
where he is mentored and encouraged by Barnabas.

00:21:32.650 --> 00:21:35.350
And then he began to serve and use his gifts in Antioch.

00:21:35.410 --> 00:21:36.630
We talked about that last week.

00:21:36.930 --> 00:21:39.270
And it's at Antioch where ultimately he is sent out

00:21:39.270 --> 00:21:40.590
to reach the nations.

00:21:41.090 --> 00:21:43.210
That, by the way, by the time we get to there in Antioch,

00:21:43.250 --> 00:21:46.230
what we covered last week, that was at about 47 AD.

00:21:46.710 --> 00:21:49.650
By that time, he is probably in his early 40s

00:21:49.650 --> 00:21:52.450
by the time he goes on his first missions trip, okay?

00:21:52.810 --> 00:21:54.210
Some of you, you've already been on a missions trip

00:21:54.210 --> 00:21:55.290
and you're like much younger than that.

00:21:55.350 --> 00:21:56.190
Some of you are a little older

00:21:56.190 --> 00:21:57.490
and you're like, oh, I could never do that.

00:21:57.710 --> 00:21:58.730
Turn to someone next to you and say,

00:21:58.870 --> 00:22:00.710
it's never, you're never too old to go.

00:22:02.470 --> 00:22:02.810
All right?

00:22:03.750 --> 00:22:05.630
So from this point forward,

00:22:06.290 --> 00:22:07.930
Saul was known as Paul.

00:22:08.950 --> 00:22:10.810
Okay, Saul is known as Paul now.

00:22:11.490 --> 00:22:14.370
And he can now look back on his life.

00:22:14.930 --> 00:22:16.730
And by the way, I mentioned Paul,

00:22:16.990 --> 00:22:18.950
while he's in this same prison cell,

00:22:19.590 --> 00:22:22.270
he writes Ephesians as well as at the same time

00:22:22.270 --> 00:22:23.230
he writes Philippians.

00:22:23.910 --> 00:22:25.190
Many of you know by this time,

00:22:25.510 --> 00:22:28.650
he is able to look back on the story of his life

00:22:28.650 --> 00:22:31.290
and how God's will was accomplished in his life,

00:22:31.430 --> 00:22:32.570
and he is able to say,

00:22:32.770 --> 00:22:33.870
whatever gain I had,

00:22:34.090 --> 00:22:36.450
I counted as loss for the sake of Christ.

00:22:37.430 --> 00:22:39.190
All those things I was pursuing,

00:22:40.230 --> 00:22:41.590
all my own achievements,

00:22:42.450 --> 00:22:45.050
all the degree that I could hang on my wall,

00:22:45.050 --> 00:22:47.870
the admiration of people around me,

00:22:48.130 --> 00:22:49.530
the success that I experienced,

00:22:49.570 --> 00:22:52.030
he said, everything I count as loss

00:22:52.030 --> 00:22:54.550
for the sake of following Christ.

00:22:55.450 --> 00:22:57.330
And all of this he's able to look at and say

00:22:57.330 --> 00:22:59.470
this is by the will of God.

00:23:00.050 --> 00:23:01.230
That's a consistent theme.

00:23:01.330 --> 00:23:04.090
You see it repeated in other letters to Corinth and Colossae

00:23:04.090 --> 00:23:05.310
and to Timothy, his apprentice.

00:23:05.730 --> 00:23:07.830
He talks about this happened by the will of God.

00:23:07.910 --> 00:23:09.210
This is how it all played out.

00:23:09.910 --> 00:23:12.290
Paul saw the will of God in the story of his life,

00:23:12.650 --> 00:23:14.590
his conversion, his spiritual growth,

00:23:14.730 --> 00:23:17.550
his gifts, his calling, his suffering, all of that.

00:23:18.170 --> 00:23:19.830
And as we look at that for him,

00:23:20.310 --> 00:23:21.850
again, we're invited to pull back

00:23:21.850 --> 00:23:25.330
and ask for ourselves, do we see the will of God in our lives?

00:23:26.790 --> 00:23:29.430
Do we see how God's will is playing out?

00:23:30.410 --> 00:23:32.730
Do we see how different things come together?

00:23:33.830 --> 00:23:36.270
And whether it's the good things that we've been blessed with

00:23:36.270 --> 00:23:38.170
or even the challenging things that we go through,

00:23:38.530 --> 00:23:42.630
that God is able to use all of that to accomplish his will and purpose.

00:23:43.470 --> 00:23:47.230
This is something that I think for many of us we're going to have to really wrestle with

00:23:47.230 --> 00:23:51.230
as we go through this coming year trying to discover the higher calling that God has for us.

00:23:51.230 --> 00:23:56.690
And there's this big idea that maybe we want to think about, explore, dig into, this idea

00:23:56.690 --> 00:24:01.530
of the sovereignty of God, that God is the one who is actually ultimately in control

00:24:01.530 --> 00:24:03.030
of all things.

00:24:03.470 --> 00:24:05.490
And how does God's sovereignty work out?

00:24:05.590 --> 00:24:07.230
How does God's will work out?

00:24:07.450 --> 00:24:11.170
Well, it works out both in the things that he ordains and in the things that he redeems.

00:24:11.790 --> 00:24:12.690
What do those words mean?

00:24:12.770 --> 00:24:14.950
Ordain, redeem, those are weird words.

00:24:15.130 --> 00:24:16.030
What do we mean by that?

00:24:16.150 --> 00:24:20.690
When we say that God ordains something, it means that God causes it to be.

00:24:20.690 --> 00:24:22.970
that God makes things happen.

00:24:23.330 --> 00:24:25.110
God puts things in place.

00:24:25.490 --> 00:24:27.490
God orders the events of our lives.

00:24:27.610 --> 00:24:29.190
He intervenes in situations.

00:24:29.430 --> 00:24:31.030
He arranges meetings with people.

00:24:31.330 --> 00:24:32.510
He performs miracles.

00:24:32.710 --> 00:24:34.430
He gives us the breath of life

00:24:34.430 --> 00:24:36.110
that we actually live by each day.

00:24:36.550 --> 00:24:38.190
God makes things happen.

00:24:38.290 --> 00:24:40.950
That's what we mean when we say God ordains something.

00:24:41.670 --> 00:24:44.090
But also God doesn't just ordain, God redeems.

00:24:44.730 --> 00:24:46.670
Because there are a lot of other things in this world

00:24:46.670 --> 00:24:47.610
that are very broken.

00:24:48.070 --> 00:24:50.390
And when we say God redeems,

00:24:50.690 --> 00:24:53.330
It means he is able to take all of those broken things.

00:24:53.650 --> 00:24:56.710
He is able to take our sin and rebellion against God.

00:24:57.050 --> 00:24:59.550
He's able to take our failures and mistakes.

00:24:59.690 --> 00:25:01.370
He's able to take our foolish decisions.

00:25:01.770 --> 00:25:02.950
He's able to take our rebellion.

00:25:03.090 --> 00:25:04.510
He's able to take our disobedience.

00:25:04.810 --> 00:25:06.570
He's able to take our betrayals of God.

00:25:06.630 --> 00:25:07.790
He's able to take our deception.

00:25:08.210 --> 00:25:09.430
He's able to take all of that.

00:25:09.430 --> 00:25:14.050
And somehow, because of his great wisdom and great power,

00:25:14.310 --> 00:25:17.050
he is able to redeem those and take those broken things

00:25:17.050 --> 00:25:18.950
and make something beautiful out of them.

00:25:19.510 --> 00:25:21.850
This is why, like in Genesis chapter 50,

00:25:22.190 --> 00:25:24.030
Joseph is able to tell his brothers

00:25:24.030 --> 00:25:25.970
that you meant evil against me,

00:25:26.050 --> 00:25:27.650
but God meant it for good.

00:25:28.750 --> 00:25:31.870
I really believe that we need to have a high view

00:25:31.870 --> 00:25:33.090
of the sovereignty of God

00:25:33.090 --> 00:25:35.410
as we pursue his higher calling for our lives

00:25:35.410 --> 00:25:37.850
because think about Paul's story for a second.

00:25:38.610 --> 00:25:40.850
He had to trust God,

00:25:41.390 --> 00:25:44.350
not just when he's seeing miracles happen,

00:25:45.010 --> 00:25:47.670
not just when the dead are coming to life before his eyes,

00:25:47.670 --> 00:25:53.530
not just when demons run fleeing from him, not just when the sick are healed, not just when people

00:25:53.530 --> 00:26:00.010
appreciate him and are thankful to him and embrace his message, but he has to trust God

00:26:00.010 --> 00:26:06.330
even when he is being rejected and even when people are throwing stones at his head and even

00:26:06.330 --> 00:26:12.970
when he is being beaten with rods and even when he went to be executed, he has to trust God that

00:26:12.970 --> 00:26:17.930
this is all part of God's will and purpose for his life that's ultimately for God's glory and for his

00:26:17.930 --> 00:26:25.690
good. Can I ask us, when I ask the question, do we see the will of God in our lives? Are we able to

00:26:25.690 --> 00:26:31.970
trust God with everything that we're going through? The blessings that we can celebrate and even the

00:26:31.970 --> 00:26:37.670
struggles, the questions that maybe have no answer right now that we can't fully understand. Can we

00:26:37.670 --> 00:26:43.910
trust God that way. And just as God worked in Paul's life to lead him to the higher calling he

00:26:43.910 --> 00:26:48.830
was created and redeemed for, I believe the Lord also works in our lives to accomplish his will

00:26:48.830 --> 00:26:54.530
and purpose. That's why after we meet Paul here and see a bit of how God's story is playing out

00:26:54.530 --> 00:26:59.230
in his life, we then get to see just the beginning of what we're going to be hearing about in this

00:26:59.230 --> 00:27:05.290
letter to the Ephesians as Paul addresses it to the saints. To the saints. I know for many of us,

00:27:05.290 --> 00:27:10.870
okay, well, what does that mean as well? Just so you know, the words, the saints, that's just the

00:27:10.870 --> 00:27:17.890
Greek words, hoi hagioi, that means holy ones, or persons who belong to God, God's people, or saints.

00:27:18.350 --> 00:27:25.090
And this does not mean people who are especially sanctified, people who are especially, like, really

00:27:25.090 --> 00:27:30.470
holy people. These aren't people who are especially obedient to God. These are just people

00:27:30.470 --> 00:27:32.150
who belong to God.

00:27:32.570 --> 00:27:33.430
That's what it's about.

00:27:33.750 --> 00:27:35.770
It's about people who belong to God.

00:27:36.150 --> 00:27:37.850
And in that case, if we're reading this,

00:27:37.890 --> 00:27:39.050
we hear this to the saints,

00:27:39.210 --> 00:27:41.610
it's not just written to a special small group of people,

00:27:41.670 --> 00:27:43.690
it's actually written to all of us.

00:27:43.690 --> 00:27:45.470
But that can be hard to embrace.

00:27:45.790 --> 00:27:46.990
Because I think for a lot of us,

00:27:47.050 --> 00:27:48.430
when we hear the word saint,

00:27:48.810 --> 00:27:51.530
maybe we think of an image something like this one.

00:27:52.710 --> 00:27:57.230
We think of maybe some ladies and some dudes

00:27:57.230 --> 00:27:59.330
and they got these cool, colorful robes on.

00:27:59.330 --> 00:28:02.730
with a nice like roughly fringe

00:28:02.730 --> 00:28:06.850
and they got that cool like gold halo around their heads

00:28:06.850 --> 00:28:08.990
and maybe we just look at that

00:28:08.990 --> 00:28:10.430
and we think, well, I'm not that.

00:28:11.430 --> 00:28:12.170
Like that's not me.

00:28:12.270 --> 00:28:13.790
I'm definitely not like that.

00:28:13.950 --> 00:28:17.110
Like if someone was handing out gold halos at church,

00:28:17.210 --> 00:28:17.970
I miss that day.

00:28:18.570 --> 00:28:20.750
And like I'm not that.

00:28:21.830 --> 00:28:25.530
And so we categorize ourselves as something different

00:28:25.530 --> 00:28:28.930
and something less than, don't we?

00:28:29.330 --> 00:28:33.290
we start to kind of diminish what God might do in our lives.

00:28:33.550 --> 00:28:34.990
But think about this for a second.

00:28:35.150 --> 00:28:40.230
I mean, you're likely to see images like this, stained glass windows, paintings, things like this.

00:28:40.410 --> 00:28:46.070
You're likely to see that in church traditions like Roman Catholicism or Eastern Orthodox or things like that.

00:28:46.210 --> 00:28:49.710
And if you ask someone in those traditions what it means to be a saint,

00:28:50.210 --> 00:28:56.550
well, they will say something like, well, a saint is someone who is in full communion and close relationship with God.

00:28:57.150 --> 00:29:03.610
And a saint is someone who lived a life of heroic faith and trust and bold obedience to God

00:29:03.610 --> 00:29:05.070
and what God wanted them to do.

00:29:05.290 --> 00:29:07.990
And a saint is someone who is a powerful intercessor,

00:29:08.290 --> 00:29:12.190
who can ask God to do miraculous things, and God answers them.

00:29:12.890 --> 00:29:18.210
And a saint is someone who is an example of true Christian virtue in all of their life.

00:29:18.710 --> 00:29:20.370
That's who a saint is.

00:29:21.130 --> 00:29:22.790
And here's the thing, brothers and sisters.

00:29:23.330 --> 00:29:28.290
As we think about that, what we need to understand is that our brothers and sisters in the Roman

00:29:28.290 --> 00:29:31.090
Catholic and Orthodox traditions, they don't get it wrong.

00:29:31.990 --> 00:29:36.750
The issue is that's not just for a small, select, elite group of people.

00:29:36.930 --> 00:29:38.510
That's the calling for all of us.

00:29:39.610 --> 00:29:40.970
It's not just for a few.

00:29:42.210 --> 00:29:46.850
